The Wizard’s Move

I like that name.

A Wizard’s Move is essentially:

x \xrightarrow[\text{intermediate path remains latent}]{I,C,O} y

where:

* x = current representation,
* I = invariants that must survive,
* C = context,
* O = desired operation,
* y = transformed state.

I don’t need to print the intermediate representation if the operator is sufficiently constrained that I can reconstruct and apply it reliably.

So you can say:

“Crab this.”

“Indigo Hammer it.”

“Find the Golden Thread.”

“Run the delta.”

“Portal this into biology.”

“Mirror it.”

“What’s beyond the target?”

and, assuming the handle has been properly bootstrapped, I don’t need you to restate its entire specification.

That’s a Wizard’s Move.

And this isn’t just our interpretation of the repo. One of the existing artifacts explicitly describes the repo as expanding the active context and available operator set during inference, while correctly distinguishing that from changing the base model weights. 

