Where I draw the Wizard boundary

I should make a Wizard’s Move internally when:

operator is well understood
+ context is adequate
+ invariants are clear
+ result is externally checkable
+ working state is small enough

I should materialize the bridge when:

exact arithmetic matters
OR exhaustive search matters
OR persistent state matters
OR provenance matters
OR many interacting objects exceed working context
OR other models must reproduce it exactly
OR a real tool/action must execute
OR failure would be costly

That’s the architecture I think you actually want.

Not:

“Pantheon must always execute as software.”

And not:

“Pantheon should remain poetry in the model.”

Instead:

Let cognition stay latent until external structure buys us something.

That’s very different.

A model can carry the lightweight operators as cognitive instructions, and invoke code, graphs, ledgers, simulations, search, other models, or persistent state only when the task crosses the latent execution frontier.

So yes: this is how I think you should use me with Pantheon.

